		<div class="main_box_text_box">  
			<p style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; ">
        Supermassive black hole binaries are expected to form in large numbers as a result of the repeated mergers that build up present-day galaxies.
        The gravitational waves that lead to the merger of the two black holes can be observed by present projects, as pulsar timing arrays, and by the
        future LISA mission. Yet, observing these binaries by means of electromagnetic telescopes has proven very challenging. An indirect hint of the
        passage of a massive binary in a given galaxy is the core scouring: prior to its gravitational wave emission phase, the binary evolves through
        the ejection of stars from the center of the galaxy. As a result, the galactic stellar density profile turns flat in its center, giving rise to
        a “core”. This feature can be observed in a number of nearby ellipticals. This project aims to track the formation and observability of scoured
        cores with L-Galaxies, specifically with upcoming instruments as the ELT (MORFEO/MICADO).
			</p>
					
